 ## Overview
-Hybrid Parallel Volume Rendering is a parallel programming model that combines distributed-memory parallelism (MPI) for inter-node communication with shared-memory parallelism (threads/GPU) for intra-node computation [S1, S2]. It solves the problem of diminishing returns and high communication costs associated with extreme concurrency in pure MPI implementations by reducing the number of MPI participants [S1, S2]. Unlike pure MPI approaches where raycasting runs serially on each task, this method multi-threads the raycasting step within each task using image-parallel decomposition, significantly improving performance, reducing memory footprint, and lowering communication bandwidth requirements [S1, S2].
+Hybrid Parallel Volume Rendering is a parallel programming model that combines distributed-memory parallelism (MPI) for inter-node communication with shared-memory parallelism (threads) for intra-node computation [S1, S2]. It solves the problem of diminishing returns and high communication costs associated with extreme concurrency in pure MPI implementations by reducing the number of MPI participants [S1, S2]. Unlike pure MPI approaches where raycasting runs serially on each task, this method multi-threads the raycasting step within each task using image-parallel decomposition, significantly improving performance, reducing memory footprint, and lowering communication bandwidth requirements [S1, S2].
 
